+---
+title: "A C++ nested type name reaches Python with its template arguments dropped"
+kind: "gotcha"
+summary: "std::list<T>::iterator is translated to std.list.iterator() — no arguments — so a nested name must be a class on _Template, not on the per-element class."
+tags: []
+created: "2026-11-21T02:55:39.732Z"
+updated: "2026-11-21T02:55:39.732Z"
+---
+When a macro declares a variable of a nested type, `xrdroot.pycint` emits the name as an attribute chain on the template *object* and drops the template arguments, so `std::list<std::pair<TBranch*,TClonesArray*> >::iterator iBranch;` becomes `iBranch = std.list.iterator()` (`std.list` is a `_Template`, not a class). Anything a macro can write after `::` must therefore exist on `_Template` in `src/xrdroot/cppstd/containers.py`, and it must be constructible with no arguments, because the arguments were never passed on. Check what the translator actually emits before designing the Python side:
+
+```
+PYTHONPATH=$PWD/src python -c "import xrdroot.pycint as c; print(c.translate(open('M.C').read(), 'M.C'))"
+```
+
+```mermaid
+graph LR
+  A["std::list<std::pair<TBranch*,TClonesArray*> >::iterator iBranch;"] -->|pycint| B["iBranch = std.list.iterator()"]
+  B --> C["_Template.iterator<br/>(cppstd/containers.py)"]
+  C --> D["_DeclaredIterator()<br/>compares, steps, refuses to read"]
+  D -->|"iBranch = branches.begin()"| E["_VectorIterator<br/>it->first, it += 1"]
+```
+
+## What this cost and what fixed it
+
+- `_Template` answered no `iterator`, so `roottest/root/io/tree/test-r` died with `AttributeError: '_Template' object has no attribute 'iterator'` before reading a branch. `_DeclaredIterator` plus `_Template.iterator` / `_Template.const_iterator` fixed it for the whole family at once (vector, list, deque, set, multiset, map, multimap, forward_list, `unordered_*`) — and `src/xrdroot/pyroot/stl.py` needs no edit, since it re-exports the same `TEMPLATES` dict.
+- One class stands for every container's iterator, because per-element specialisation is impossible without the arguments. That is why the "default-constructed" iterator can only compare, step and be replaced by a `begin()`; reading through one refuses with a full sentence instead of failing on an index.
+- A declared iterator is only half the feature: `std::set`/`std::multiset` had no `begin()`/`end()` at all, so check for those too when a macro declares an iterator of a container you have not exercised. `_SetIterator` counts off `_Set._ordered()` because a set has no position to index.
+- `printf("%d", *it)` passes the iterator itself to the runtime's `printf` (that is how `*it` is emitted), so the iterator needs `__int__` as well as the `__float__` that `%g` and `cout` use.
